{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-blog-post-tsx","path":"/blog/2023-12-26--reflections-on-advent-of-code-2023/","result":{"data":{"site":{"siteMetadata":{"disqus":"edward-j-schwartz"}},"post":{"htmlAst":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hello, Merry Christmas and Happy Holidays!  I just completed "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://adventofcode.com/2023"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advent of\nCode 2023"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and wanted to jot down a few reflections\nbefore I disappear for the holidays.  Like "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"/blog/2023-01-24--initial-thoughts-on-rust/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"last\nyear"}]},{"type":"text","value":", I chose to "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/advent-of-code-2023/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"implement my\nsolutions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" using Rust."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h3","properties":{"id":"rust-easier-with-practice-but-still-a-bit-in-the-way","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#rust-easier-with-practice-but-still-a-bit-in-the-way","ariaLabel":"rust easier with practice but still a bit in the way permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Rust: easier with practice, but still a bit in the way"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The good news is that I ran into far fewer problems with Rust this year.  There\nare a few reasons for this, but a lot of it just boils down to experience.  I\ntend to program excessively using lazy iterators, and I began to get a second\nsense for when I'd need to call "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":".collect()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to avoid referencing a freed\ntemporary value.  Similarly, when I would get a compiler error (and yes, this\nstill happens a lot!), I would often immediately know how to fix the problem."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"That being said, there were still occasions when the language just \"got in the\nway\".  I had a few solutions where I had to throw in copious amounts of\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":".clone()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" calls.  One salient example was on "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/advent-of-code-2023/blob/master/day22/src/main.rs#L119"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"day\n22"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nwhen I wanted to memoize a subproblem.  But to do so, I also had to pass a value\ninstead of a reference to each sub-problem, which slowed things down\ndramatically.  Anyway, with more work, the solution probably would have been to\nuse references with lifetimes and just ensure that memoization happened during\nthe right lifetimes.  But the key phrase there is "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"with more work"}]},{"type":"text","value":".  And see\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#frustration-and-clear-headedness-the-fine-line-between-perseverance-and-stubbornness"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"below"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n-- I was already pretty frustrated."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h3","properties":{"id":"chat-gpt-and-co-pilot","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#chat-gpt-and-co-pilot","ariaLabel":"chat gpt and co pilot permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Chat-GPT and Co-pilot"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"On this year's Advent of Code, I used Chat-GPT and Co-pilot quite heavily, as I\ndo in practice when coding.  I used Chat-GPT mostly to avoid looking up\ndocumentation or crates.  For example, \"How can I add a progress indicator to an\niterator in Rust?\" or \"How can I bind a sub-pattern to a name in Rust?\".  Given\nthat I hadn't programmed in Rust for a year, I was a bit rusty (ha!)"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Co-pilot was also quite useful.  It was pretty good at writing out boilerplate\ncode.  Advent of Code problems tend to include copious amounts of grid problems,\nand Co-pilot reduced a lot of the fatigue for helper functions.  Define things\nfor the X axis, and it would figure out how to implement the other cases."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I also found that Co-pilot was able to explain weird Rust borrowing problems far\nbetter than the compiler.  As I wrote about last year, Rust's borrowing rules\nsometimes don't work that great for my programming style, but Co-pilot was\npretty good about explaining problems.  I don't have a specific example, but\nthink of searching a vector using "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"vec.iter()"}]},{"type":"text","value":", using the found value at some\npoint, and then later trying to move the vector.  In my opinion, this definitely\nmakes it easier to program in Rust."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now, Co-pilot did introduce some headaches as well.  Although it was pretty\ngood, it would sometimes introduce problematic completions.  For instance, it\nreally liked to complete print statements by adding an extra quote, e.g.,\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"println!(\"This is something: {:?}\", something\");"}]},{"type":"text","value":"  Not a huge deal, but it\nhappened enough that it was irritating."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h3","properties":{"id":"frustration-and-clear-headedness-the-fine-line-between-perseverance-and-stubbornness","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#frustration-and-clear-headedness-the-fine-line-between-perseverance-and-stubbornness","ariaLabel":"frustration and clear headedness the fine line between perseverance and stubbornness permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Frustration and Clear-headedness: The Fine Line Between Perseverance and Stubbornness"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"On some level, I think everyone knows this, but it's still worth saying: You are\nmore effective when thinking clearly.  I recall from graduate school that many\nof my fellow students would pull all nighters and submit papers in the wee hours\nof the morning.  I never did that.  My efficiency just plummets as I get tired."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I experienced a pretty funny (in retrospect) example of this on "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://adventofcode.com/2023/day/22"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"day\n22"}]},{"type":"text","value":".  You can read the problem, it's not\nreally that hard.  One of the nice things about programming functionally is that\nit usually works or completely fails.  There's not a lot of in-between.  In\nAdvent of Code, this means that if your solution works on the example, it\nusually works on the real input (ignoring performance).  But on day 22, my code\ndid not work on the real input.  The problem is pretty simple, so it was\ninfuriating.  Rather than taking a break to clear my head, I just kept trying to\ndebug it.  I stayed up far past my bed time, becoming more and more frustrated.\nI even completely rewrote my implementation in Python before giving up and going\nto bed."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The next morning, I woke up and looked at the problem again.  I had a simple\nidea.  I actually had two solutions to the problem.  I had a simple but slow\nsolution that I was confident was correct, but was too slow to use on the real\ninput. And I had my fast solution that I thought should be correct, but wasn't.\nMy insight was that I could make \"in between\" problems by just taking a portion\nof the real input -- say 10%.  And then see if my fast solution agreed with the\nslow one.  It didn't.  I then ran\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/MozillaSecurity/lithium"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lithium"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and reduced the input to a\nminimal example.  From here, it was trivial to spot the fundamental problem I\nhad in my recursive algorithm.  I fixed it, and my solution worked.  This whole\nprocess probably only took 30 minutes from the time I woke up.  I futilely spent\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"hours"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the previous night.  And the worst part is that, even in the moment, I\nknew I was acting foolishly.  I was just too frustrated to stop."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I don't know if this is universal, but when I am stressed or frustrated, I usually\nmake mistakes, which naturally compound my stress and frustration!  There is a\nfine line between perseverance and stubbornness, and I crossed it on day 22."}]}],"data":{"quirksMode":false}},"excerpt":"Hello, Merry Christmas and Happy Holidays!  I just completed Advent of\nCode 2023, and wanted to jot down a few reflections\nbefore I…","timeToRead":4,"fields":{"slug":"/blog/2023-12-26--reflections-on-advent-of-code-2023/"},"frontmatter":{"tags":["programming","rust","adventofcode"],"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","bio":"Computer Security Researcher","twitter":"@edmcman","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/4ed6d/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/4ed6d/ed.png 80w","sizes":"80px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/1deab/ed.webp 80w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"80px"}]},"width":80,"height":80}}}},"title":"Reflections on Advent of Code 2023","updatedDate":"Dec 26, 2023","image":null}},"recents":{"edges":[{"node":{"id":"1ee49631-39a1-507e-a660-6790ad897154","fields":{"slug":"/blog/2026-05-15--commonsense-benchmarks-for-local-coding-agents/"},"timeToRead":7,"frontmatter":{"title":"auto-bench: Benchmarking Quantized LLMs for Local Coding Agents","image":null,"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/b0e74/ed.png 72w","sizes":"36px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/9a807/ed.webp 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/de323/ed.webp 72w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"36px"}]},"width":36,"height":36}}}}}}},{"node":{"id":"6add3982-894c-5ae3-8eb5-e486d8415c0e","fields":{"slug":"/blog/2026-01-15--idioms-a-simple-and-effective-framework-for-turbo-charging-local-neural-decompilation-with-well-defined-types-accepted-to-ndss-2026/"},"timeToRead":4,"frontmatter":{"title":"\"Idioms: A Simple and Effective Framework for Turbo-Charging Local Neural Decompilation with Well-Defined Types\" Accepted to NDSS 2026","image":null,"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/b0e74/ed.png 72w","sizes":"36px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/9a807/ed.webp 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/de323/ed.webp 72w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"36px"}]},"width":36,"height":36}}}}}}},{"node":{"id":"1830d226-8c63-581b-ac33-ae67fe1e45bd","fields":{"slug":"/blog/2025-12-06--musings-on-decompilation-artifacts/"},"timeToRead":3,"frontmatter":{"title":"Musings on Decompilation Artifacts","image":null,"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/b0e74/ed.png 72w","sizes":"36px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/9a807/ed.webp 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/de323/ed.webp 72w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"36px"}]},"width":36,"height":36}}}}}}},{"node":{"id":"d85be7de-c5b3-5be8-a80f-23e9f7a3feeb","fields":{"slug":"/blog/2025-11-06--alias-blocking-in-ghidra-s-decompiler/"},"timeToRead":23,"frontmatter":{"title":"Alias Blocking in Ghidra's Decompiler","image":null,"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/8ef0e/ed.png 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/b0e74/ed.png 72w","sizes":"36px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/9a807/ed.webp 36w,\n/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/de323/ed.webp 72w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"36px"}]},"width":36,"height":36}}}}}}}]}},"pageContext":{"slug":"/blog/2023-12-26--reflections-on-advent-of-code-2023/"}},"staticQueryHashes":[],"slicesMap":{}}